cat > README.md <<'EOF1'
# Google Helps

Node/Express backend + SQLite + a mobile-first website (installable PWA). The Android app is the same site packaged as a Trusted Web Activity, so there is one codebase to maintain.

## What works
- Accounts (bcrypt + JWT), saved chat history, daily free credits, invite links (+10 credits each)
- Chat with Gemini and/or Llama via Groq (free tiers). A model shows up only if its key is set.
- Code answers with syntax highlighting and Copy buttons; text/code file attach (first 10,000 characters)
- Image generation (Pollinations, free, no key)
- Voice input and read-aloud (browser Web Speech; support varies by browser)
- Dark/light mode, feedback form, rate limiting, helmet security headers, API keys stay on the server

## Not built (so there are no fake buttons)
- Video generation: no reliable free text-to-video API exists. Add a paid one later; the credit system is ready for it.
- Paid plans: needs Google Play Billing (Android) and a web payment provider. Credits and limits are shown, but there is no Upgrade button yet.
- Code editor / app-builder workspace and saved "projects": chat history is the only saved work for now.

## Run it
1. `cp .env.example .env`, set `JWT_SECRET` (`node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"`).
2. Get free keys: Gemini at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey, Groq at https://console.groq.com. Paste one or both into `.env`. Check the providers' docs for current model names and free limits.
3. `npm install && npm start`, then open http://localhost:3000

## Test it
```
curl localhost:3000/health
curl -X POST localhost:3000/api/register -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"email":"a@b.co","password":"password123"}'
# copy the token, then:
curl -X POST localhost:3000/api/chat -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H "Authorization: Bearer TOKEN" -d '{"message":"Say hi"}'
```
Rate limit check: send 21 bad logins in 15 minutes and you should get HTTP 429. If chat returns 502, read the server log: it prints the provider's error (bad key, wrong model name, quota).

## Deploy
SQLite needs a disk that survives restarts. Free hosts that wipe the disk will delete your users. Use a small VPS (roughly $5/month) or a host with a persistent disk. Steps on a VPS: install Node 20+, copy the folder, create `.env`, run with `pm2 start server.js`, put Caddy or Nginx in front for HTTPS. HTTPS is required for the PWA, the microphone, and Play. Back up `data.db` regularly. If you outgrow SQLite, move to Postgres.

## Publish on Google Play
1. Deploy to your real HTTPS domain first.
2. Go to https://www.pwabuilder.com, enter your URL, choose Android, and download the package. It contains your signing key. Back it up.
3. Put the `assetlinks.json` it gives you at `https://YOUR-DOMAIN/.well-known/assetlinks.json` (add a `public/.well-known` folder; Express serves dotfolders only with `dotfiles: 'allow'` in `express.static`).
4. Create a Play Console account (one-time fee) and upload the .aab. New personal accounts currently must run a closed test with testers before production; check Play's current rules.
5. Required: privacy policy URL, Data safety form, content rating. Say that chat text is sent to Google (Gemini) and/or Groq, and that image prompts go to Pollinations. Free-tier terms can allow providers to use inputs to improve their products, so read them and disclose it.
6. Name warning: "Google" in an app name can get rejected or taken down for implying affiliation. Consider a different name before you publish.
7. Store listing: put the main keyword in the title ("... AI Code Fixer & Chat"), a clear first line of the description, real screenshots.

## Growth hooks already in the code
Landing page text for SEO (title, description, Open Graph), invite links, daily credits, starter prompts for beginners, feedback form. Add a sitemap and a privacy page next.
